  "enhanced_information": "The 11-year-old girl presents with frothy brown urine, elevated serum creatinine, hematuria with RBC casts, and elevated urine protein, alongside a recent history of sore throat and fever. These findings suggest glomerular involvement, particularly post-infectious glomerulonephritis. The presence of hematuria with RBC casts is indicative of glomerular damage, which aligns with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, a common consequence of streptococcal infections. Alport syndrome is less likely due to the absence of family history and hearing loss. Minimal change disease is unlikely given the hematuria, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis is less common in this age group with this presentation. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, supported by the recent infection and clinical findings."
